# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Private planning material (kept local, not part of the public write-up)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
archive/
plan/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vendor firmware is NOT redistributed. Only provenance metadata is committed
# (firmware/MANIFEST.json), so a reader can fetch and hash-verify the exact
# same image without this repository hosting copyrighted binaries.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.web
*.bin
*.img
*.trx
*.squashfs
*.rom
# Vendors ship firmware inside archives, and the extension of the *container*
# is what a browser saves. W04-2 nearly redistributed one: a browser-downloaded
# N150RT-V2.1.6-*.zip landed in study/, which no rule here matched, and it was
# one `git add -A` from being committed. The rules above all named the payload;
# none named the wrapper.
*.zip
*.rar
*.7z
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
firmware/*
!firmware/.gitkeep
!firmware/SOURCES.json
!firmware/MANIFEST.json
!firmware/README.md

# Flash / UART dumps from my own device: raw images stay local, hashes are committed.
dumps/*
!dumps/.gitkeep
!dumps/MANIFEST.json
!dumps/README.md
# W04-2: the console transcripts are published. MANIFEST records
# contains_unit_identifiers:false for both, and that was re-checked against the
# files rather than taken from the manifest before they were added.
!dumps/*.log
# and the raw images are still blocked, by a rule that is deliberately NOT
# relaxed alongside the one above. See dumps/README.md: the two reasons for
# keeping them out were written as independent, and only one of them expired.
dumps/*.bin

# Unpacked filesystems — large, and reconstructible from the manifest + tooling.
extracted/
squashfs-root*/
_*.extracted/

# Ghidra project state (multi-GB, machine-specific). Scripts are committed.
ghidra/projects/
*.gpr
*.rep/

# Decompiled C exported by ghidra/scripts/BoaDecompile.java.
# Decompiler output is a derivative of the vendor binary, so committing the whole
# corpus would redistribute the firmware by another route — see "Scope & ethics"
# in README.md. Short excerpts quoted inside an analysis, with commentary, are a
# different thing and those are committed under notes/.
ghidra/decomp/

# Generated reports ARE committed. They are the readable product of this work,
# and a reader without the (non-redistributable) firmware should still be able
# to see what the analysis found. `make recon` regenerates them byte-for-byte
# from an image whose hash is in firmware/MANIFEST.json.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Language / editor
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
dist/
build/
.coverage
htmlcov/

.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
